                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#ReferencesOpenSlices"} Open spatial slices or not?
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#both_or_neither"} Both or neither?
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What has been called
            %em Witten’s Dark Fantasy
            (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HeckmannLawrieLinZoccarato19"} Heckmann, Lawrie, Lin &amp; Zoccarato 19, Section 8
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95a"} Witten 95a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95b"} Witten 95b, Sec. 3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten00"} Witten 00, p. 7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            %em non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            4d
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacua"} string vacua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            with fundamentally
            %em vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (i.e. vanishing “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"}> dark energy
            ”).
          %p
            The original idea was formulated in terms of 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+8-manifolds"} M-theory on 8-manifolds
            decompactified at strong coupling to 4d via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
            (recall the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+2-brane+in+4d"} super 2-brane in 4d
          %p
            Based on an observation of
            %a{:href => "#Vafa96"} Vafa 96, Section 4.3
            that the argument should have a natural realization in 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} F-theory on Spin(7)-manifolds
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-dual
            to the previous perspective), a detailed construction was finally laid out in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BonettiGrimmPugh13"} Bonetti-Grimm-Pugh 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HeckmannLawrieLinZoccarato118"} Heckmann-Lawrie-Lin-Zoccarato1 18
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HeckmanLawrieLinSaksteinZoccarato19"} Heckman-Lawrie-Lin-Sakstein-Zoccarato 19
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#supersymmetry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/2
            \-Supersymmetry
          %p
            The key technical point is the claim that a careful analysis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D4+N%3D1+supergravity"} D=4 N=1 supergravity
            obtained after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} F-theory on Spin(7)-manifolds
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-dual to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} M-theory on Spin(7)-manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+of+supersymmetries"} N=1 supersymmetry
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory+on+CY4-folds"} F-theory on CY4-folds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N= 1/2
            supersymmetry”, where:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
                and hence has vanishing
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %li
              %p
                but no
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
                = succeed "-excitation" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
              %p
                hence
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosons"} bosons
                in the model do
                %em not
                appear in supersymmetric spectra.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vafa96"} Vafa 96, Sec. 4.3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BonettiGrimmPugh13"} BGP 13
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HeckmannLawrieLinZoccarato118"} HLLZ 18, Sec. 4
          %h3#ClosedSpatialSlices Closed spatial slices
          %p
            The concrete realization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten%27s+Dark+Fantasy"} Witten's Dark Fantasy
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            of
            %a{:href => "#HeckmannLawrieLinZoccarato118"} Heckmann-Lawrie-Lin-Zoccarato1 18
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            where spatial slices are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            and in fact of the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+topology"}> cosmic
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/PixelatedSpatialSliceInWittenDarkFantasyModel.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics from
              %a{:href => "#HeckmanLawrieLinSaksteinZoccarato19"} Heckman-Lawrie-Lin-Sakstein-Zoccarato 19
          %h3#Phenomenology Phenomenology
          %p
            When the idea of “Witten’s Dark Fantasy” was proposed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95a"} Witten 95a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95b"} Witten 95b
            %a{:href => "#Witten00"} Witten 00
            it was right before observation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+shifts"} red shifts
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supernovae"} supernovae
            convinced cosmologists, in 2001, of a relatively small but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            fell out of favor, and a vocal sub-community instead embarked on arguing that
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+spacetime"} de Sitter spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacua"} string vacua
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            had to be searched at random in a large
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %p
            However, debate remains over whether the apparently observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            is actually real:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The authors of
                %a{:href => "#KLKCR19"} KLKCR 19
                claim that temporal evolution of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supernovae"} supernovae
                luminosity had been underappreciated, which makes the apparent evidence for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
                completely go away. Earlier,
                %a{:href => "#NielsenGuffantiSarkar16"} NGS 16
                had pointed out that even with the established interpretation of the data, a vanishing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
                is not excluded by the data.
            %li
              %p
                Since around 2000 authors have argued that the apparent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
                may be an artifact of the usual
                = succeed "-cosmologies" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} cosmic inhomogeneities
                into account. The situation with this debate currently remains open (see at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} inhomogeneous cosmology
              %p
                While it is uncontroversial that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} cosmic inhomogeneity
                does have a measurable effect on cosmic expansion, the general current consensus seems to be that it is too small to explain all of the dark energy of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SNLuminosityEvolutionCancelsApparentCC.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a{:href => "#KLKCR19"} KLKCR 19
          %p
            In the extreme case, if re-analysis of the data, combined with effects of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} cosmic inhomogeneity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+curvature+corrections"} higher curvature corrections
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (such as control the observationally preferred
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Starobinsky+model+of+cosmic+inflation"} Starobinsky model of cosmic inflation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %em Witten’s dark fantasy
            would again appear to be viable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
          %p
            It is then interesting to notice that also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            spatial slices found in the model
            %a{:href => "#ClosedSpatialSlices"} above
            have recently been argued to be preferred by observational data (
            %a{:href => "#VMS19"}> VMS 19
            ).
          %p
            That available experimental data supports neither a cosmological constant nor open spatial slices is further argued in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ValentinoMelchiorriSilk20"} Di Valentino-Melchiorri-Silk 20
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"}> cosmology
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} inhomogeneous cosmology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %h2#references References
          %h3#theory Theory
          %h4#general_idea General idea
          %p The idea in rough form goes back to
          %ul
            %li#Witten95a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong coupling and the cosmological constant
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9506101"} arXiv:hep-th/9506101
            %li#Witten95b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , Section 3 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some Comments On String Dynamics
                %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/305869"} Strings95
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9507121"}> arXiv:hep-th/9507121
                )
            %li#Witten00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , p. 7 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Cosmological Constant From The Viewpoint Of String Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/972507"} DM2000
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sources and detection of dark matter and dark energy in the universe 2000
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0002297"} arXiv:hep-ph/0002297
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-662-04587-9"} doi:10.1007/978-3-662-04587-9
          %p
            The observation that the idea should naturally embed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} F-theory on Spin(7)-manifolds
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Vafa96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"}> Cumrun Vafa
              , Section 4.3 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Evidence for F-Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9602022"} arxiv:hep-th/9602022
          %h4#DetailedImplementation Detailed implementation
          %p
            A detailed implementation of the idea in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} F-theory on Spin(7)-manifolds
            is developed in:
          %ul
            %li#BonettiGrimmPugh13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Federico+Bonetti"}> Federico Bonetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Grimm"} Thomas Grimm
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Pugh"} Tom Pugh
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Non-Supersymmetric F-Theory Compactifications on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Spin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 7
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(7)
                    Manifolds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.5858"} arXiv:1307.5858
            %li#BonettiGrimmPugh13b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Federico+Bonetti"}> Federico Bonetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Grimm"} Thomas Grimm
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eran+Palti"} Eran Palti
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Pugh"} Tom Pugh
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    F-Theory on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Spin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 7
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(7)
                    Manifolds: Weak-Coupling Limit
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.2287"} arXiv:1309.2287
            %li#HeckmannLawrieLinZoccarato19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Heckman"}> Jonathan Heckman
                , Craig Lawrie, Ling Lin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gianluca+Zoccarato"} Gianluca Zoccarato
                = succeed "," do
                  %em F-theory and Dark Energy
                %strong 67
                10 (2019) 1900057 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01959"}> arXiv:1811.01959
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.201900057"} doi:10.1002/prop.201900057
            %li#HeckmanLawrieLinSaksteinZoccarato19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Heckman"}> Jonathan Heckman
                , Craig Lawrie, Ling Lin, Jeremy Sakstein,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gianluca+Zoccarato"} Gianluca Zoccarato
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Pixelated Dark Energy
                %strong 67
                11 (2019) 1900071 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.10489"}> arXiv:1901.10489
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.201900071"} doi:10.1002/prop.201900071
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+J.+Heckman"}> Jonathan J. Heckman
                , Omar F. Ramadan, Jeremy Sakstein,
                %em First Constraints on a Pixelated Universe in Light of DESI
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04408"}> arXiv:2406.04408
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesPhenomenology Phenomenology
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            = succeed "-density" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            and preferred
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"}> spherical
            ) spatial slices of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten%27s+Dark+Fantasy"} Witten's Dark Fantasy
          %p
            It may very well be that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten%27s+Dark+Fantasy"} Witten's Dark Fantasy
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologicaly
            unviable. But it is interesting to notice that there is recent and very recent astrophysical analysis which claims problems with exactly these two aspects of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten%27s+Dark+Fantasy"} Witten's Dark Fantasy
            is exactly the kind of model needed to match observation.
          %h4#ReferencesDarkEnergy Dark energy or not?
          %p
            Argument that the observed type Ia
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supernovae"} supernovae
            are actually consistent with a vanishing
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %ul
            %li#NielsenGuffantiSarkar16
              %p
                J. T. Nielsen, A. Guffanti,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Subir+Sarkar"} Subir Sarkar
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Marginal evidence for cosmic acceleration from Type Ia supernovae
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01354"} arXiv:1506.01354
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://4gravitons.wordpress.com/2016/11/11/a-response-from-nielsen-guffanti-and-sarkar/"} web discussion
            %li#ColinMoymhayeeRamezSarkar19
              %p
                Jacques Colin, Roya Mohayaee,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mohamed+Rameez"} Mohamed Rameez
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Subir+Sarkar"} Subir Sarkar
                %em A response to Rubin &amp; Heitlauf: “Is the expansion of the universe accelerating? All signs still point to yes”
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.04257"}> arXiv:1912.04257
                )
          %p
            Stronger argument that the observed type Ia
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supernovae"} supernovae
            in fact
            %em prefer
            a vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (due to time-dependency of SN brightness that had been missed):
          %ul
            %li#KLKCR19
              %p
                Yijung Kang, Young-Wook Lee, Young-Lo Kim, Chul Chung, Chang Hee Ree,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Early-type Host Galaxies of Type Ia Supernovae. II. Evidence for Luminosity Evolution in Supernova Cosmology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.04903"} arXiv:1912.04903
              %p exposition:
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://phys.org/news/2020-01-evidence-key-assumption-discovery-dark.amp?__twitter_impression=true"} New evidence shows that the key assumption made in the discovery of dark energy is in error
          %h4#ReferencesOpenSlices Open spatial slices or not?
          %p
            Arguments that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+collaboration"} PLANCK satellite
            data actually prefers a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            spatial slices (contrary to the assumption in the current
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %ul
            %li#Handley19
              %p
                Will Handley,
                %em Curvature tension: evidence for a closed universe
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09139"}> arXiv:1908.09139
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/1751120"} spire:1751120
            %li#VMS19
              %p
                Eleonora Di Valentino, Alessandro Melchiorri, Joseph Silk,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Planck evidence for a closed Universe and a possible crisis for cosmology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02087"} arXiv:1911.02087
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0906-9"} doi:s41550-019-0906-9
          %p A critique of these arguments is given in
          %ul
            %li
              George Efstathiou, Steven Gratton,
              %em The evidence for a spatially flat Universe
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.06892"}> arXiv:2002.06892
              )
          %p
            but this critique again rests on just the combination
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+collaboration"} Planck collaboration
            \&amp; baryon acoustic peak (BAO) &amp;
            = succeed "-data" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supernova"} supernova
          %p
            From the abstract of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Handley19"} Handley 19
          %blockquote
            %p The curvature parameter tension between Planck 2018, cosmic microwave background lensing, and baryon acoustic oscillation data is measured using the suspiciousness statistic to be 2.5 to 3σ. Conclusions regarding the spatial curvature of the universe which stem from the combination of these data should therefore be viewed with suspicion. Without CMB lensing or BAO, Planck 2018 has a moderate preference for closed universes, with Bayesian betting odds of over 50:1 against a flat universe, and over 2000:1 against an open universe.
          %h4#both_or_neither Both or neither?
          %p
            Combined argument that experimental data disfavours both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (favoring instead effective
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phantom+dark+energy"} phantom dark energy
          %ul
            %li#ValentinoMelchiorriSilk20
              Eleonora Di Valentino, Alessandro Melchiorri, Joseph Silk,
              %em Cosmic Discordance: Planck and luminosity distance data exclude LCDM
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04935"}> arXiv:2003.04935
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
